$ patslint --help
usage: patslint [option | file] ...

Check the style of ATS source files (.sats, .dats, .hats).

options (an option applies to the files that follow it):
  -h, --help           print this help
  --list-rules         print the rules and their default severities
  --enable=RULE,...    turn rules on (at their default severity)
  --disable=RULE,...   turn rules off
  --severity=RULE:SEV  set the severity of a rule
                       (off, info, warning, error)
  --max-col=N          the maximal length of a line (default 80)
  --indent-width=N     the unit of indentation (default 2)
  --lines=N-M,...      report only the findings on these lines
  --all-lines          report the findings on all the lines (default)
  --fail-on=SEV        the least severity that makes the exit status 1
                       (default warning)
  --format=text|json   the output: one finding per line, text or JSON
  --source             (text) each finding is followed by its line and
                       a caret under its column (the default when the
                       output is a terminal)
  --no-source          (text) the findings only
  --fix                fix trailing-ws, crlf, final-newline, blank-eof
                       and the tabs of indentations in place
  --semantic           also the rules after the binding of the names
                       (unused-var, ...; slower: the prelude is loaded)

The nearest .patslint file in the directory of a file or above it
configures its check (lines of the form KEY = VALUE, # for comments):
  RULE = off | on | info | warning | error
  max-col = N
  indent-width = N
  exclude = GLOB       (relative to the directory of .patslint)
The options of the command line take precedence over it.
A comment [lint: disable=RULE,...] disables rules on its line and the
next one, [lint: disable-file=RULE,...] in the whole file (RULE can
be [all]).
exit: 0
